 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can reduce the number of samples based on render time constraints [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the total size of the volume textures and transfer functions uploaded to GPU memory. Limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Performance depends on GPU hardware and resolution. Adaptive sampling helps maintain frame rates for large datasets [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales with the number of pixels (threads) and volumes. Limited by `MAX_NUMBER_OF_ADDITIONAL_VOLUMES` (3-5) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Hardware acceleration via GLSL shaders, support for multiple volumes, adaptive sampling, and masking [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited number of additional volumes, potential slowdown from `ReportProgress` (glFinish) on small datasets, and non-zero extent workaround requirements [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering multiple aligned or transformable volume datasets on the GPU with VTK [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When more than `MAX_NUMBER_OF_ADDITIONAL_VOLUMES` volumes are needed, or when non-GPU rendering is required [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. Adaptive sampling can reduce the number of samples based on scene complexity.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the size of the volume data and the number of volumes. Limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes`.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance depends on GPU hardware, resolution, and scene complexity. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can improve performance by reducing sample density in less detailed areas.
+*   **Scaling:** Scales with the number of GPU cores. Rendering multiple volumes adds overhead due to additional texture lookups and compositing steps.
+*   **Strengths:** Hardware acceleration, support for multiple volumes, adaptive sampling, masking.
+*   **Limitations:** Limited by GPU memory, hardware loop limits require nested while loops, non-zero extents require workaround, cropping planes with zero/negative volume fail silently.
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering multiple overlapping volumes is required, and hardware acceleration is available.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When GPU memory is insufficient, or when rendering very small datasets where CPU-based rendering might be faster due to overhead.
 
 ## Background
-The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension to the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian from CTIM [S1, unofficial]. The project uses GLSL shaders instead of OpenCL for general GPU programming because shaders are faster and supported by more devices [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` by adding support for multiple inputs, coordinate transformations, and compositing [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension of the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` to support the simultaneous rendering of multiple volume datasets. It was implemented using GLSL shaders for general GPU programming, chosen for their speed and broader device support compared to OpenCL [S1, unofficial]. The project includes modifications to the VTK ray casting method, specifically in the `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper_CompositeFS` and `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper_ShadeFS` shaders, to handle multiple volumes and their transformations [S1, unofficial].
 
